/* Generic UNIX device list
/*                                                  
special   special   00 00  x x x x 15  UNKNOWN              Special request
xwindow   xdriver   01 00  x x x   15  con                  X-window driver
xterm     tektrx    10 00  x x x x 01  FILE*/dev/tty        Xterm/tek4014
ps        postdrv   01 01          07  PIPE*"lpr -h"        Postscript on default
pogo      postdrv   01 01          07  PIPE*"lpr -Ppogo -h" Postscript to "pogo"
lucy      postdrv   01 01          01  PIPE*"lpr -Plucy -h" Postscript to "lucy"
psdisk    postdrv   06 01          01  FILE*query           Postscipt Landscape
pstex     postdrv   07 01          01  FILE*query           Postscipt Portrait
hp300     laserjet  01 00          01  pipe*"lpr -Popus -h" Laser to printer
deskjet   deskjet   01 00          01  pipe*"lpr -Pwhatever -h"
quietjet  quietjet  01 00          01  pipe*"lpr -Pquietjet -h"
hp7475    hp-gl     05 01  x x     06  file*%tmp%           HP 7475 to file
colorpro  hp-gl     07 01  x x     06  disk*"/dev/tty7"     HP ColorPro on TTY7
hpdisk    hp-gl     06 01  x x     06  file*query           HP 7550 disk output
tiff      tiff      05 256         01  file*query           72 DPI Tiff file
tiff300   tiff      01 256         01  file*query           300 DPI Tiff file
ncsa      tektrx    02 00  x x x x 01  FILE*/dev/tty        NCSA Telnet?
hrz       tektrx    06 00  x x x x 01  FILE*/dev/tty        Hirez Terminal
go235     tektrx    06 00  x x x x 01  FILE*/dev/tty        GraphOn 235
tkc       tektrx    03 00  x x x x 15  FILE*/dev/tty        syn for 4105
tkem      tektrx    09 00  x x x x 15  FILE*/dev/tty        syn for 4105
tek4010   tektrx    02 00  x x x x 01  FILE*/dev/tty        Tek4010 devices
tek4105   tektrx    03 00  x x x x 15  FILE*/dev/tty        Tek4105 terminal
kermit    tektrx    08 00  x x x x 01  FILE*/dev/tty        Kermit terminal 
retro     tektrx    05 00  x x x x 01  FILE*/dev/tty        Retrographics
rem_hrz   tektrx    06 00          01  FILE*/dev/hirez      Remote hirez
linus     hp-gl     06 01          08  XON*PIPE*"lpr -Plinus -h" HP7550
linust    hp-gl     06 01          08  XON*PIPE*"lpr -Plinust -h" HP7550 overheads
hpdisk    hp-gl     06 01  x x     06  XON*FILE*query       HP 7550 disk output
hpb       hp-gl     05 01          06  XON*FILE*/dev/tty    HP7475 on terminal
hpd       hp-gl     06 01          08  XON*PIPE*"lpr -Phps -h" HP7550
debug     nuldrv    02 00      x x 08  FILE*/dev/tty  Debug device
null      nuldrv    01 00      x x 08  FILE*/dev/tty  Null device
@END           
---------------------------------------------------------------------
| D N     DEVICE     D  S |H|M|C|R| N  OUTPUT           Description |
| E A     DRIVER     E  E |A|A|R|E| U  CHANNEL                      |
| V M     NAME       V  C |S|Y|T|S| M  STRING                       |
| I E                I  O | | | |E| B                               |
| C                  C  N |C|H|T|R| E  see                          |
| E                  E  D |U|A|Y|V| R  list                         |
|                       A |R|V|P|E|    below                        |
|                    T  R |S|E|E|D| P                               |
|                    Y  Y |O| | | | E                               |
|                    P    |R|C|S| | N                               |
|                    E  P | |U|C| | S                               |
|                       A | |R|R| |                                 |
|                       R | |S|E| | I                               |
|                       M | |O|E| | 2                               |
|                         | |R|N| |                                 |
----------------------------------------------

This file contains the specifications for device names, and the internal
driver codes associated with each name.  The format of this file is NOT
flexible.  It is hard coded to be: 

   1) 8 char device name + 2 blanks         (A8,2X)       1-8     9-10
   2) Executable device driver              (A8,2X)      11-18   19-20
   3) Device type (see driver listings)     (I2,1X)      21-22      23
   4) Secondary parm (see drivers)          (I2,2X)      24-25   26-27
   6) device characteristics - bits         4(A1,1X)     28-34   29-35
   7) number of pens available (< 16)       I2           36-37   38-39
   5) Output channel (see below)            (A40)        40-xx
   8) description  (see note)               Remainder    xx-80

123456789 123456789 123456789 123456789 123456789 123456789 123456789 12345678
dddddddd  nnnnnnnn  ii ii  x x x x ii  pppppppppppp ---- descriptive text ----

Note: The I/O channel can use the remainder of the line up to 40 characters.
      A single blank character terminates the I/O string and the remainder of
      the line is a descriptor comment only.

The specific device type numbers and the secondary parameters are
specific to each driver and the appendix concerning drivers should be
consulted to fill in these fields.

The generally recognized output devices are listed below.  Specific drivers
may either ignore these names or implement others of it's own.

SCREEN                                  - Screen 
CON                                     - Console (debug)
ASYNC*COM1*ENQ    ASYNC*COM2*ENQ        - COM 1/2 w/ ENQ/ACK protocol
ASYNC*COM1*XON    ASYNC*COM2*XON        - COM 1/2 w/ XON/XOFF protocol
ASYNC*COM2*DTA    ASYNC*COM2*DTR        - COM 1/2 w/ hardward protocol
PRN                                     - Default printer
LPT1        LPT2        LPT3            - LPT1: etc.
SPOOL*COM1*info  SPOOL*COM2*info        - Spool to ASYNC*COM1, ASYNC*COM2
DISK                                    - Generates T$xxxx.PLT disk files
DISK*<name>                             - Generates file with <name>
DISK*QUERY                              - Queries at execute time for filename
DISK*LPT1                               - Same as LPT1 above
GPIB*<name>                             - IEEE488 w/ device <name>

Notes: 
   1) The special and null devices should always be included in the code.
   2) The '/*' as first two chars of a line indicate a comment.
   3) @END as a line indicates logical end of file.  Remainder of the file is
      considered to be comment text.
   4) WARNING: The Fortran compiler assumes a TAB to occupy a single space.
      They MUST not be used in this file!
   5) XTERM emulator requires the xterm*tekGeometry field in .Xdefaults.
      Suggested value is 600x600+200+200.
